# No project-specific content in code or configuration

The AI Assistant plugin must work on **any** Crafter site. **Never** bake in identifiers or copy from a particular project, demo, customer site, or test fixture.

## Forbidden in Groovy, JSON recipes, prompts, schemas, and config

- **Field element ids** (e.g. `hero_text_html`, `sections_o` as a assumed default target)
- **Content-type paths** tied to one blueprint (e.g. `/page/home` as the only page type)
- **Repository paths** for one site (`/site/website/index.xml` as implicit default beyond Studio anchor context)
- **Demo copy / topics** (vehicle names, branded tip titles, lorem from a specific QA site)
- **Snippet lists** for version matching invented from one conversation (“Research and Documentation”, product names)
- **Fallback labels** that assume one form (“hero title” when the author did not say a field label)

## Allowed (site-agnostic mechanisms)

- Read **Studio context** the request already provides: anchored `Repository path`, `content-type id`, `siteId`, form appendix.
- Resolve field targets via **`GetContentTypeFormDefinition`** + author **label phrases** (map `<title>` → `<id>` for *that* item’s type at runtime).
- **Tool arguments** the model supplies after **`GetContentVersionHistory`** / **`GetContent`** (`contentContains`, `version`, `contentFieldId` from form def — not guessed in Java/Groovy).
- **Natural-language intent patterns** that match *author wording* (`revert`, `summarize this page`, `add … to … field`) without naming a specific element id.
- **Generic** examples in tool descriptions (“distinct phrases from the version you want”, “element id from GetContentTypeFormDefinition”).

## Revert / field-edit hotpaths

- Hotpaths may only use **generic flags**: `revertToInitial`, `revertToPrevious`, explicit `version`, or tool args from the model.
- **Do not** infer `contentContains` or `contentFieldId` from project-specific heuristics in server code.

## Before merging orchestration or recipe changes

- Grep the diff for known anti-patterns: concrete `_html` / `_t` field ids, demo product names, site nicknames, hardcoded XML paths used as logic branches.
- If a feature needs site-specific behavior, it belongs in **site** `tools.json`, custom recipes, or **ui.xml** — not in the plugin bundle.

False positives from demo-tuned logic waste maintainer time and break real customer sites. When in doubt, push resolution to **tools + form definition + author message**, not hardcoded branches.
